# slideSonnet showcase narration — deck: showcase.pdf
# A two-voice dialog: Alex (:voice alex) asks, the narrator (default) answers.
# This narration was written in exactly this format and rendered by the tool.

@title
Welcome to slideSonnet. Everything you're about to hear was written as plain
text in a sidecar file, and rendered straight from a PDF. Let me show you how.
[pause 0.5]

@intro-q
:voice alex
So what does slideSonnet actually do?

@intro-a
You bring a finished PDF of your slides, and slideSonnet turns it into a narrated
video. The narration you're hearing right now lives in a plain text file, sitting
right next to that PDF.

@why-q
:voice alex
Why not just put the narration inside the slides themselves?

@why-a
Because the PDF is your frozen artifact. Keeping narration in a separate,
git-diffable file means you can rewrite a line and re-render in seconds. No
recompiling the slides, no re-recording a microphone.

@markers
Step one: mark your slides. Add the slideSonnet package to your Beamer source,
and give every slide a stable id with the ess-ess-eye-dee macro. The id gets
stamped invisibly onto each page, so narration binds to the id, not to a page
number that shifts when you insert a slide.

@init
Step two: scaffold the sidecar. Run slidesonnet init on your PDF, and it reads
the ids straight out of the document and writes a blank narration file, one block
per slide, ready for you to fill in. By hand, or by an LLM.

@format
The format is simple. An at-sign and the slide id starts a block. Optional voice
and pace directives go on lines starting with a colon. And square-bracket pause,
with a number, inserts silence. That one primitive covers a pause between
sentences, a hold at the end of a slide, or an entirely silent slide.

@edit-q
:voice alex
Do I really have to write that text file by hand?

@edit-a
You can, or you can run slidesonnet edit for a local editor. Page through the
deck, type narration beside each slide, and preview the whole thing. The preview
bakes in your pauses, so it plays back exactly like the final video will.

@export
Step four: render. Run slidesonnet export with the piper engine for free local
speech, or elevenlabs for high quality cloud voices. Want a fast rough cut? Use
silent mode, or estimate timing, to skip speech entirely. Subtitles are written
alongside the video automatically.

@cache-q
:voice alex
Doesn't re-rendering everything get slow?

@cache-a
Audio is cached by content. Edit one block, and only that line is
re-synthesized. Everything else is reused. And swapping piper for elevenlabs
never throws away the audio you already paid for.

@recap
That's the whole workflow. Mark your slides, scaffold the narration, check that
the ids line up, write and preview, then export. Every step is scriptable, so an
LLM or a Makefile can drive the entire pipeline without ever opening the editor.

@closing
Mark your slides. Write narration. Render. Give slideSonnet a try. [pause 1]
